  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d6242805-a43c-55a0-a9db-4f603170dc1b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 e^{- 4 x}$\n- **II.** $y=- 2 e^{- 5 x}$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 e^{- 5 x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 e^{- 4 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 e^{- 4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\\\ - 12 e^{- 4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(3 e^{- 4 x}\\right) \\\\ - 12 e^{- 4 x} & =- 12 e^{- 4 x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 e^{- 4 x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 2 e^{- 5 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10 e^{- 5 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\\\ 10 e^{- 5 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(- 2 e^{- 5 x}\\right) \\\\ 10 e^{- 5 x} & \\overset{?}{=}8 e^{- 5 x} \\\\ 2 e^{- 5 x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 2 e^{- 5 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 e^{- 5 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=15 e^{- 5 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\\\ 15 e^{- 5 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{- 5 x}\\right) \\\\ 15 e^{- 5 x} & \\overset{?}{=}12 e^{- 5 x} \\\\ 3 e^{- 5 x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 e^{- 5 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
