    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ec5a8f80-fc54-597b-9eb0-be013f4e7fab",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=-1 + \\frac{4}{x^{2}}$\n- **II.** $y=2 + \\frac{2}{x^{2}}$\n- **III.** $y=- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-1 + \\frac{4}{x^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{8}{x^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{8}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-2\\cdot \\left(-1 + \\frac{4}{x^{2}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{8}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{x} - \\frac{8}{x^{3}} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-1 + \\frac{4}{x^{2}}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 + \\frac{2}{x^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{4}{x^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-2\\cdot \\left(2 + \\frac{2}{x^{2}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}- \\frac{4}{x} - \\frac{4}{x^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 + \\frac{2}{x^{2}}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- \\frac{1}{x^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-2\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{x^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
