    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9c008f70-bfdc-5fc6-ae7c-1030dc558109",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)},\\,\\sin(3 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y=1 - 4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & =6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 - 4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - 12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(1 - 4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ - 12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\left(1 - 4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{\\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 - 4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1\\right) \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
