  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b4af0a9e-da6b-5f20-a4df-9bdd3e2b1888",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=1 + e^{- 2 x}$\n- **II.** $y=-2 + 4 e^{- 2 x}$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 e^{- 2 x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=1 + e^{- 2 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 e^{- 2 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 y \\\\ - 2 e^{- 2 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2\\cdot \\left(1 + e^{- 2 x}\\right) \\\\ - 2 e^{- 2 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 - 2 e^{- 2 x} \\\\ 2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 + e^{- 2 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2 + 4 e^{- 2 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 8 e^{- 2 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 y \\\\ - 8 e^{- 2 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2\\cdot \\left(-2 + 4 e^{- 2 x}\\right) \\\\ - 8 e^{- 2 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4 - 8 e^{- 2 x} \\\\ -4 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2 + 4 e^{- 2 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 e^{- 2 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 e^{- 2 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 y \\\\ 6 e^{- 2 x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{- 2 x}\\right) \\\\ 6 e^{- 2 x} & =6 e^{- 2 x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 3 e^{- 2 x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3d528a22-67f2-5fd9-b26e-7b3b569769c8",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
