  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6310601d-13ab-5a7a-a181-0d586509692c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 y \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)},\\,\\cos(4 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2$\n- **II.** $y=3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ - 8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2\\right)\\tan\\left(4 x\\right) \\\\ - 8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 8 \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ 8 \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ - 12 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right)\\tan\\left(4 x\\right) \\\\ - 12 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & =- 12 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=16 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 y \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ 16 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-4\\cdot \\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\tan\\left(4 x\\right) \\\\ 16 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}16 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 4 \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\\\ - 4 \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
