  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e62af4a3-dff8-5598-abe3-cfe357246588",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{3 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=\\frac{4}{x^{3}}$\n- **II.** $y=1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}$\n- **III.** $y=-1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{x^{3}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{12}{x^{4}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{12}{x^{4}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{x^{3}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{12}{x^{4}} & =- \\frac{12}{x^{4}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{x^{3}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{6}{x^{4}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{x^{4}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-3\\cdot \\left(1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{x^{4}} & \\overset{?}{=}- \\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{6}{x^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{6}{x^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{-3\\cdot \\left(-1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{6}{x^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{9}{x^{3}} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{3}{x^{3}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
